{
  "title": "Solving x² − 5x + 6 = 0",
  "lang": "en",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "The Equation & Method 1",
      "say": "We want to solve x squared minus 5x plus 6 equals 0. Our first instinct might be the quadratic formula.",
      "ops": [
        { "op": "write", "id": "t_main", "x": 80, "y": 50, "text": "Solve: x² − 5x + 6 = 0", "size": 52 },
        { "op": "write", "id": "m1_hdr", "x": 80, "y": 140, "text": "Method 1: Quadratic Formula", "size": 38, "color": "blue" },
        { "op": "write", "id": "m1_coeff", "x": 80, "y": 200, "text": "a = 1,   b = -5,   c = 6", "size": 32, "color": "gray" },
        { "op": "write", "id": "m1_form", "x": 80, "y": 260, "text": "x = (-b ± √(b² - 4ac)) / (2a)", "size": 34 }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Evaluating the Formula",
      "say": "Substituting the coefficients gives 5 plus or minus the square root of 25 minus 24, all divided by 2.",
      "ops": [
        { "op": "write", "id": "m1_sub", "x": 80, "y": 330, "text": "x = (5 ± √(25 - 24)) / 2", "size": 34 },
        { "op": "write", "id": "m1_disc", "x": 80, "y": 400, "text": "x = (5 ± 1) / 2", "size": 34 },
        { "op": "write", "id": "m1_ans", "x": 80, "y": 470, "text": "⇒  x = 3   or   x = 2", "size": 36, "color": "blue" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Wait—Is There a Simpler Way?",
      "say": "The formula works, but look how much arithmetic we did. Notice the discriminant was a clean square!",
      "ops": [
        { "op": "rect", "id": "box_notice", "x": 70, "y": 550, "w": 650, "h": 120, "color": "red", "width": 3 },
        { "op": "write", "id": "t_stop", "x": 95, "y": 575, "text": "Wait: this is overkill!\nThe coefficients are small integers.\nCan we factor directly instead?", "size": 28, "color": "red" },
        { "op": "line", "id": "arrow_shift", "x1": 740, "y1": 340, "x2": 850, "y2": 340, "color": "green", "width": 5, "arrow": true }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Method 2: Factoring by Inspection",
      "say": "Let's factor directly. We just need two numbers that multiply to positive 6 and add up to negative 5.",
      "ops": [
        { "op": "write", "id": "m2_hdr", "x": 880, "y": 140, "text": "Method 2: Factoring (Simpler!)", "size": 38, "color": "green" },
        { "op": "write", "id": "m2_rule", "x": 880, "y": 210, "text": "Find two numbers p and q such that:", "size": 32, "color": "ink" },
        { "op": "write", "id": "m2_p_mul", "x": 920, "y": 270, "text": "•  p × q = +6", "size": 34, "color": "blue" },
        { "op": "write", "id": "m2_p_add", "x": 920, "y": 330, "text": "•  p + q = -5", "size": 34, "color": "blue" },
        { "op": "write", "id": "m2_pair", "x": 880, "y": 410, "text": "The pair is:  -2  and  -3", "size": 36, "color": "green" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Instant Solution",
      "say": "Now rewrite the quadratic as (x - 2)(x - 3) = 0. The roots are immediately 2 and 3.",
